**November 18, 2026 — Thermal performance above T~opt~ vs drug tolerance (Script 32: `R-Scripts-Joey/32-above-topt-vs-high-drug.R`)**

*Question:* Do strains that perform better at supra-optimal temperatures also tolerate antifungal drugs better?

*Thermal metrics (per strain, from the fitted Sharpe-Schoolfield TPCs; gcplyr AUC growth response; `data-processed/gcplyr/tpc-params-auc-gcplyr-17.csv` and `tpc-predictions-auc-gcplyr-17.csv`, which match the Script 19 fits):*
- **Mean performance above T~opt~ (primary; `mean_above_topt`):** integral of the fitted TPC from the strain's own T~opt~ to its own T~max~, divided by (T~max~ − T~opt~). This is the average fitted performance across all temperatures between the optimum and the upper limit. Range 0.533–0.626.
- **Area above T~opt~ (secondary; `auc_above_topt`):** the integral itself, not divided by window width.
- Integration: trapezoid rule on the 500-point prediction grid (15–50°C), with T~opt~ and T~max~ added as endpoints by linear interpolation.
- What they capture (within-group correlations): the area is almost entirely window width (r = 0.99 with T~max~ − T~opt~). Mean performance largely tracks peak growth (r = 0.77 with r~max~), so it reflects overall growth level as well as how well growth holds up above the optimum.
- The window is strain-specific: T~opt~ ranges 37.0–40.1°C and T~max~ 42.0–44.0°C, so strains are averaged over different temperature ranges.

*Drug metrics (per strain; all normalized to the same-plate ancestor):*
- **log~2~ IC~50~ ratio:** `log_ratio` from `normalised-ic50-per-strain.csv` (11d; min-RSS fits, no exclusions), i.e. log~2~(IC~50~ / same-plate ancestor IC~50~), averaged over plates.
- **Growth at high drug (`high_drug_lfc`):** relative growth (OD / zero-drug OD in the same row) as log~2~ fold change vs the same-plate ancestor at the same concentration, log~2~((rel + 0.05) / (rel~anc~ + 0.05)). Averaged over plates, then over the four doubling steps above the ancestor's median IC~50~ (pooled across plates): fluconazole 32–256 µg/mL (ancestor IC~50~ 16.4), caspofungin 0.031–0.25 µg/mL (0.016), amphotericin B 0.5–4 µg/mL (0.35). Source: `dr-difference-curves-29.csv` (`log2_fc`).
- The two drug metrics are strongly related within groups: r = 0.68 (fluconazole), 0.82 (caspofungin), 0.81 (amphotericin B); all p < 0.001.

*Model:* drug metric ~ thermal metric + evolution history (shared slope, group intercepts); within-group partial r = t / √(t² + df). Unadjusted p-values (no multiple-comparison correction). Run on all 36 strains.

*Results — mean performance above T~opt~ (within-group partial r, p):*

| Drug | IC~50~ (n = 36) | High-drug growth (n = 36) |
|---|---|---|
| Fluconazole | 0.46, 0.005 | 0.54, 0.001 |
| Caspofungin | 0.47, 0.005 | 0.11, 0.53 |
| Amphotericin B | 0.48, 0.003 | 0.47, 0.004 |

*Results — other thermal metrics vs log~2~ IC~50~ (all strains):* T~h~: r = 0.30 (fluconazole, p = 0.08), 0.14 (caspofungin, p = 0.43), 0.23 (amphotericin B, p = 0.19). T~opt~: 0.22, −0.03, 0.02 (all p ≥ 0.21). T~max~: 0.03, 0.14, 0.21 (all p ≥ 0.23). Area above T~opt~ vs high-drug growth: r = 0.17–0.21, all p ≥ 0.22.

*Interpretation:*
- Performance across supra-optimal temperatures is positively associated with fluconazole and amphotericin B tolerance (by both drug metrics) and with caspofungin IC~50~. Thermal limits (T~opt~, T~max~, T~h~) and the width of the above-optimum window are not.
- The difference from the earlier null IC~50~ results comes from the thermal metric, not the drug metric: the earlier tests used T~opt~, T~max~ and T~h~ (where the curve sits), whereas mean performance above T~opt~ reflects how high the curve is.
- The window lies between each strain's T~opt~ and T~max~, so the metric is a measure of heat-stress performance: how well a strain grows across the range where growth declines because of temperature. Strains with better heat-stress performance are more drug-tolerant. The metric also tracks r~max~ (r = 0.77), so it reflects overall growth level within that range. This matches Script 31: fluconazole tolerance at high drug correlates with growth at 25–38°C (r = 0.48–0.61) and more weakly at 41–42°C (r ≈ 0.3, n.s.).
- In the IC~50~ figure, amphotericin B trends positively within both evolution groups. The fluconazole panel includes the March Set2 35-evolved cluster (35_D2, D8, E7, E9, E11; ≈ +1.5 log~2~). These strains have stable IC~50~s across the three Set2 plates (≈ 11–19), but the same-plate ancestor IC~50~ varies ~6-fold (Rep1 1.92, Rep2 12.1, Rep3 4.80), so their same-plate ratios are high on Rep1/Rep3 and near 0 on Rep2 (see Nov 16 caveat). 35_D4 (−1.3) is different: its own IC~50~ is consistently low on all three plates (1.7–2.2), so it is a genuinely fluconazole-sensitive strain, not an ancestor artefact.

*Caveats:*
- This metric was chosen after many exploratory cross-tolerance analyses (Scripts 23–31), so these associations should be treated as findings to confirm rather than pre-specified tests.
- At high caspofungin and amphotericin B, the 35-evolved strains sit close to the ancestor (log~2~ FC ≈ 0) because most strains are fully inhibited, so the high-drug-growth tests are effectively tested within the 40-evolved strains. For caspofungin, IC~50~ is the more informative drug metric.
- Not yet checked: fluconazole IC~50~ association without the March Set2 strains; pseudocount sensitivity (0.02, 0.1) of the high-drug metric.

*r~max~ vs mean performance above T~opt~ (Script 32 section 3c; `ic50-rmax-vs-above-topt-32.csv`):* log~2~ IC~50~ ~ r~max~ + group, ~ mean above T~opt~ + group, and both together. The two thermal metrics correlate within groups at r = 0.77. Within-group partial r (p):

| Drug | r~max~ alone | Mean above T~opt~ alone | Both: mean above T~opt~ | Both: r~max~ |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Fluconazole | 0.43 (0.010) | 0.46 (0.005) | 0.23 (0.20) | 0.13 (0.48) |
| Caspofungin | 0.53 (0.001) | 0.47 (0.005) | 0.10 (0.56) | 0.31 (0.076) |
| Amphotericin B | 0.64 (< 0.001) | 0.48 (0.003) | −0.02 (0.92) | 0.48 (0.004) |

- Amphotericin B: the association is with peak growth, not performance above T~opt~. r~max~ is the stronger predictor (0.64 vs 0.48) and carries the whole association in the joint model.
- Caspofungin: also mainly r~max~ (joint model r = 0.31, p = 0.076).
- Fluconazole: the two metrics can't be separated (both n.s. in the joint model).

*Fixed-window metric (ancestor window; `mean_fixed_window` in Script 32, `supra_fixed` in Script 33):*
- **Definition:** mean fitted growth for each strain over the ancestor's own T~opt~ to T~max~ (39.4–42.2°C), i.e. the same temperatures for every strain. Computed as the trapezoid integral of the strain's fitted TPC over that window divided by its width (2.8°C).
- **Why:** the strain-specific mean averages each strain over a different temperature range (T~opt~ 37.0–40.1°C, T~max~ 42.0–44.0°C), so a strain is compared with others at different temperatures. The fixed window compares all strains at identical temperatures and does not depend on each strain's T~opt~ or T~max~ estimates.
- **Relation to other metrics (within-group correlations):** r = 0.48 with r~max~ (vs 0.77 for the strain-specific mean), 0.54 with the strain-specific mean, and −0.06 with the strain-specific AUC.
- **Between-group differences (Welch, `stats-supraoptimal-33.csv`):** 40-evolved mean 0.684 vs ancestor 0.592 (p < 0.001); 35-evolved 0.504 vs ancestor (p < 0.001); 40 vs 35 (p < 0.001). Unlike the strain-specific mean, this metric clearly separates the 40°C-evolved strains from the ancestor, consistent with heat adaptation (the 40-evolved windows moved to higher temperatures, and their fixed-window performance rose with them).
- **Within-group association with IC~50~ (partial r, p):**

| Drug | Ancestor window (n = 36) | Strain-specific mean (n = 36) |
|---|---|---|
| Fluconazole | 0.42 (0.012) | 0.46 (0.005) |
| Caspofungin | 0.28 (0.10) | 0.47 (0.005) |
| Amphotericin B | 0.42 (0.012) | 0.48 (0.003) |

- **Interpretation:** strains that grow better at 39–42°C tend to have higher fluconazole and amphotericin B IC~50~s within groups. The association is weaker than for the strain-specific mean. There is no between-group cross-tolerance: the 40-evolved strains grow much better at these temperatures but do not differ from the 35-evolved strains in IC~50~. The link is only among strains within a group.
- **Caveat:** the fixed window is a choice (the ancestor's T~opt~ to T~max~). Other windows could give different results, and the association was not checked after adjusting for r~max~.

