*Decision (Nov 16):* Results and analysis are kept as they are: same-plate ancestor normalisation, min-RSS fits, no exclusions, and the six-variable PCA. The caveats below are recorded for reference and for responding to reviewers; they do not change any reported numbers.

*Caveat — March Set2 ancestor fluconazole (found Nov 16):* The ancestor's fluconazole IC50 varies about 6-fold across the three March Set2 replicates (Rep1 1.92, Rep2 12.1, Rep3 4.80). Rep1 and Rep3 are two of the rule-flagged shallow curves. Five 35-evolved strains measured on these plates (35_D2, 35_D8, 35_E7, 35_E9, 35_E11) have consistent IC50s of about 11–19 on all three plates, but their log2 ratios vary with the ancestor: 2.7–2.9 on Rep1, −0.04 to 0.57 on Rep2, and 1.2–2.0 on Rep3. They form a tight cluster at about +1.05 (group-centred) in per-strain fluconazole tolerance. Compared with the Rep2 ancestor alone, they are close to ancestral. This plate is likely a main driver of the 35°C-evolved fluconazole result not holding in the sensitivity analysis.

**Manuscript text (plate-normalized IC50 results, uncorrected p-values; updated November 16, 2026; min-RSS fitting, no curves excluded):**

Methods: Dose-response curves were fitted with a four-parameter logistic model from a grid of 20 starting values (five IC~50~ × four slope values), retaining the fit with the lowest residual sum of squares. All 441 curves converged and none were excluded. Each of the 378 evolved-strain plate observations was normalized to the ancestor IC~50~ measured on the same plate, and plate-level log~2~ ratios were averaged per population.

After plate normalization, both evolved groups showed modestly elevated fluconazole tolerance relative to the ancestor, whereas neither group differed from the ancestor in caspofungin or amphotericin B tolerance, and the two groups did not differ from each other for any drug (Figure 2G). For fluconazole, IC~50~ was 1.39-fold higher than the ancestor in the 35°C-evolved group (one-sample *t*-test on plate-normalized log~2~[IC~50~/ancestor IC~50~], *n* = 18 populations, *p* = 0.021, 95% CI for fold change: [1.06, 1.82]) and 1.39-fold higher in the 40°C-evolved group (*n* = 18, *p* = 0.014, 95% CI: [1.08, 1.80]); the two groups were indistinguishable (40°C/35°C: 1.00-fold; Welch *t*-test, *n* = 18 per group, *p* = 0.985, 95% CI: [0.70, 1.44]). Neither group differed from the ancestor in caspofungin tolerance (35°C-evolved: 1.06-fold, *p* = 0.406, 95% CI: [0.92, 1.21]; 40°C-evolved: 0.95-fold, *p* = 0.474, 95% CI: [0.82, 1.10]), and the two groups did not differ from each other (40°C/35°C: 0.90-fold; *p* = 0.272, 95% CI: [0.74, 1.09]). Neither group showed a change in amphotericin B tolerance relative to the ancestor (35°C-evolved: 1.06-fold, *p* = 0.264, 95% CI: [0.95, 1.19]; 40°C-evolved: 0.95-fold, *p* = 0.519, 95% CI: [0.79, 1.13]) or relative to each other (40°C/35°C: 0.89-fold; *p* = 0.251, 95% CI: [0.72, 1.09]). The fluconazole results remained significant after Holm correction within drug (both groups: *p* = 0.042) but not after Holm correction across all nine tests (35°C-evolved: *p* = 0.170; 40°C-evolved: *p* = 0.126; Table SX); we therefore interpret them as suggestive evidence of a modest increase in fluconazole tolerance shared by both selection regimes, rather than a temperature-specific effect.

